## Part 1: Introduction to interactive databases

In 2010, the team at ProPublica started with a simple idea: Collect all the payments that pharmaceutical companies make to doctors. Then build a searchable database that lets the public look up how much money any physician in America has received.

Pulling it off was more complicated. [Dan Nguyen](https://danwin.com), [Charles Ornstein](https://www.propublica.org/people/charles-ornstein) and [Tracy Weber](https://www.propublica.org/people/tracy-weber) had to find and download countless payment disclosures from a baffling array of different websites, then spend hours standardizing the inconsistent data. It was months of work before they could even begin to think about posting it online.

When ["Dollar for Docs"](https://projects.propublica.org/docdollars/) finally launched, the response was overwhelming. Readers searched it tens of millions of times. Dozens of newsrooms used the data to report their own stories. Law enforcement used it to investigate drug companies.

<Screenshot
  src="/screenshots/week-5/dollars-for-docs.png"
  alt="ProPublica's Dollars for Docs database showing a search interface where users can look up doctors by name and see pharmaceutical company payments"
  chromeTitle="Dollars for Docs"
  chromeUrl="https://projects.propublica.org/docdollars/"
  maxWidth="960px"
/>


The project was so influential that Congress eventually required drug makers to disclose their payments to [a unified federal database](https://openpaymentsdata.cms.gov/), which made the news organization's efforts obsolete.

Examined from a technical perspective, "Dollar for Docs" is an example of a distinct type of news application. You start with a public dataset. You pull it into a web application. You give readers tools to explore it themselves. The formula works for [government bailouts](https://projects.propublica.org/coronavirus/bailouts/), [preventive power outages](https://www.sfchronicle.com/projects/pge-shutoff-power-outages-map/), [money given to political candidates](https://www.opensecrets.org/), [complaints made against police officers](https://data.cpdp.co/data/bxyXG5/citizens-police-data-project) or anything else that the public wants to know about.

Regardless of the topic, the underlying architecture is the same. You have a dataset drawn from outside your code. You write a script to fetch and reshape it. You pull it into your page using a framework's data loading pattern. You render individual records with loops and templates. You use inputs, like the ones we covered [last week](/docs/coding-the-news/scripts/week-4/), to make the page interactive.

We'll cover all of those fundamentals today, step by step, using a public dataset published by New York City.

## Part 2: Fetching data with Node.js

Up to now, all of the data in our projects has been typed by hand. Last week, we hardcoded streaming prices and tip amounts directly into our components. Real projects are powered by real data — from spreadsheets, databases, PDFs and APIs — that get pulled in before the page is built.

Today we're going to learn how to bring external data into a Svelte project, where we can use it however we want. We'll use a dataset you see evidence of every day in New York City: The restaurants inspection grades posted in the window of food establishments all across the five boroughs.

The New York City Department of Health and Mental Hygiene posts an updated dataset nearly every day on the city's [open data portal](https://data.cityofnewyork.us/Health/DOHMH-New-York-City-Restaurant-Inspection-Results/43nn-pn8j/about_data), where you can download all inspections over the past three years of restaurants that remain open.

<Screenshot
  src="/screenshots/week-5/nyc-open-data-restaurant-inspections.png"
  alt="NYC Open Data portal showing the DOHMH New York City Restaurant Inspection Results dataset page with metadata and download options"
  chromeTitle="DOHMH New York City Restaurant Inspection Results"
  chromeUrl="https://data.cityofnewyork.us/Health/DOHMH-New-York-City-Restaurant-Inspection-Results/43nn-pn8j/about_data"
  maxWidth="960px"
/>

The full dataset contains nearly 300,000 records, each representing an administrative action taken by the city, covering far more than just the final letter grade.

Rather than download the whole thing, we'll write a small Node.js script that talks to the city's data server and asks for exactly the records we want. This is a common pattern in data journalism — you write a script that fetches, filters and shapes your data before it ever touches your project.

Let's start by creating a new repository from our template. Open your browser and navigate to <a href="https://github.com/palewire/cuny-jour-static-site-template" target="_blank">github.com/palewire/cuny-jour-static-site-template</a>.

<Screenshot
  src="/screenshots/week-2/template-repo-homepage.png"
  alt="GitHub template repository page showing the Use this template button"
  chromeTitle="cuny-jour-static-site-template"
  chromeUrl="https://github.com/palewire/cuny-jour-static-site-template"
/>

Click the green "Use this template" button and name your new repository `my-first-data-components`. Make sure "Public" is selected, then click "Create repository."

Clone it to your computer, open it in Visual Studio Code. Run `npm install` in the terminal.

Next, create a new file in the root of your project called `fetch-data.js`. Paste in the following code. It's a Node.js script that will hit the city's data API, ask for the last 50,000 letter grades and then save the most recent grade for each restaurant to a JSON file in our project.

```js
// Node's built-in file system module — lets us write files to disk
import fs from "fs";

// The NYC Open Data API endpoint for restaurant inspections
const apiUrl = "https://data.cityofnewyork.us/resource/43nn-pn8j.json";

// Query parameters sent to the API using the Socrata Query Language (SoQL)
const params = new URLSearchParams({
  $limit: 50000, // max number of rows to return
  $where: "grade IN('A', 'B', 'C')", // only include graded inspections
  $select: "camis,dba,boro,cuisine_description,inspection_date,grade,score", // only fetch the columns we need
  $order: "camis ASC, inspection_date DESC", // sort by restaurant ID, newest inspection first
});

// Combine the base URL and query string into one complete request URL
const url = `${apiUrl}?${params.toString()}`;

console.log(`Fetching data from NYC Open Data...`);

// fetch() makes an HTTP GET request to the API and returns a Promise
fetch(url)
  // Parse the response body as JSON — also returns a Promise
  .then((response) => response.json())
  .then((data) => {
    console.log(`Fetched ${data.length} records from the API.`);

    // A Set lets us track which restaurant IDs (camis) we've already seen,
    // so we can keep only the most recent inspection per restaurant
    const seen = new Set();
    let restaurants = data.filter((r) => {
      // If we've seen this camis before, skip it
      if (seen.has(r.camis)) return false;
      // Otherwise, mark it as seen and keep it
      seen.add(r.camis);
      return true;
    });

    // Sort by inspection_date descending so the newest inspections appear first
    restaurants.sort(
      (a, b) => new Date(b.inspection_date) - new Date(a.inspection_date),
    );

    console.log(`Filtered to ${restaurants.length} unique restaurants.`);

    // Filter to inspections on or after Jan 1, 2025
    const cutoff = new Date("2025-01-01");
    restaurants = restaurants.filter(
      (r) => new Date(r.inspection_date) >= cutoff,
    );

    console.log(
      `Filtered to ${restaurants.length} inspections on or after Jan 1, 2025.`,
    );

    // Write the cleaned data to a JSON file so our app can use it
    // JSON.stringify with null, 2 formats the output with 2-space indentation
    fs.writeFileSync(
      "src/lib/data/restaurants.json",
      JSON.stringify(restaurants, null, 2),
    );
    console.log("Saved to src/lib/data/restaurants.json");
  })
  // If anything goes wrong (network error, bad JSON, etc.), log it
  .catch((error) => {
    console.error("Error fetching data:", error);
  });
```

We won't go over everything it does here. The important thing to understand is that our work as a page designer is starting with a data file that comes from the outside world, which we'll be asked to bring to life.

Before we can run the script, we need to create the folder where it will save the data. In the terminal, make a new directory for our data file:

```bash
mkdir -p src/lib/data
```

Now run the script.

```bash
node fetch-data.js
```

You should see a message reporting how many raw records were downloaded and how many unique restaurants remain. Open `src/lib/data/restaurants.json` in Visual Studio Code and take a look. You'll see an array of objects, each representing one restaurant with its most recent letter grade. The most recent decisions should be at the top.

```json
  {
    "camis": "40369165",
    "dba": "REIF'S TAVERN",
    "boro": "Manhattan",
    "cuisine_description": "American",
    "inspection_date": "2026-02-25T00:00:00.000",
    "grade": "A",
    "score": "9"
  },
  {
    "camis": "40385852",
    "dba": "SAM'S PLACE",
    "boro": "Manhattan",
    "cuisine_description": "Italian",
    "inspection_date": "2026-02-25T00:00:00.000",
    "grade": "A",
    "score": "10"
  },
```

Scroll to the bottom and you should see inspections from early January 2025, which is the cutoff we set in our script.

```json
  {
    "camis": "50015659",
    "dba": "HARRY'S NEW YORK BAR",
    "boro": "Manhattan",
    "cuisine_description": "American",
    "inspection_date": "2025-01-02T00:00:00.000",
    "grade": "A",
    "score": "12"
  }
```

### Loading data the SvelteKit way

We have our data file. Now we need to get it into our page.

SvelteKit has an official pattern for getting data into your pages. It's called a [load function](https://svelte.dev/docs/kit/load), and it lives in a special file called `+page.js` that sits right next to your `+page.svelte`.

Before your page component renders, SvelteKit runs the load function to prepare whatever data the page needs. The page then receives that data as a property. This separates getting the data from displaying it, which keeps your component focused on presentation.

This is the pattern used by extremely complicated news applications with multiple data sources, authentication, pagination and more. It's also the pattern used by our simple project, because it's important to learn it from the start.

Create a new file at `src/routes/+page.js`. This file must be named exactly `+page.js` — SvelteKit looks for this specific filename.

```svelte
import restaurants from '$lib/data/restaurants.json';

export function load() {
  return {
    restaurants
  };
}
```

This is an extremely simple example of a `+page.js`. The first line imports our JSON data file, as it would any other Node.js library.

The `load` function is the special function that SvelteKit calls before rendering the page. Whatever object you return from it becomes available to your page component.

<Aside>
In this case, we're returning an object with one property: `restaurants`, which contains the full array of inspection records from our JSON file.

Because the name of the variable we imported matches the name of the key in our `{}` object, we can use shorthand syntax and just write `restaurants` instead of `restaurants: restaurants`. That's a JavaScript quirk that can be confusing at first.
</Aside>

Now open `src/routes/+page.svelte`. At the top of the script block, we need to receive the data that the load function prepared. Replace the existing props line or add this new one:

```js
let { data } = $props();
```

The `data` prop is another SvelteKit convention. It automatically contains whatever your `+page.js` load function returned. So `data.restaurants` is our array of inspection records.

Let's verify it works. Add a `$inspect()` call right after the props line. It's the special Svelte function that logs a variable to the browser console.

```js
$inspect(data.restaurants);
```

Start the development server with `npm run dev` and open your browser to [localhost:5173](http://localhost:5173). Open the browser's developer tools — you can do this by right-clicking anywhere on the page and selecting "Inspect," then clicking the "Console" tab.

You should see your data array logged in the console. Click the arrow to expand it and explore individual records. This is a useful trick for understanding any dataset — log it, expand it, poke at it.

Once you've confirmed it's working, you can remove the `$inspect()` line. We won't need it anymore.

## Part 3: Looping through lists with `{#each}`

You have data flowing into your page. Now what? The whole point is to show it to readers. And since we have hundreds or thousands of records, we need a way to render them all without writing each one by hand.

That's where Svelte's [`{#each}`](https://svelte.dev/docs/svelte/each) block comes in. It loops over an array and renders one row at a time. It's the tool you'll reach for anytime you need to display a list, a table, a grid of cards, or any other repeating pattern.

Inside the `ArticleBody` in `+page.svelte`, add a HTML table that uses an `{#each}` block to repeat the same pattern for each row in `data.restaurants`.

```svelte
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Restaurant</th>
      <th>Borough</th>
      <th>Cuisine</th>
      <th>Grade</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    {#each data.restaurants as row}
      <tr>
        <td>{row.dba}</td>
        <td>{row.boro}</td>
        <td>{row.cuisine_description}</td>
        <td>{row.grade}</td>
      </tr>
    {/each}
  </tbody>
</table>
```

Inside the block, we use the familiar curly-brace syntax to insert each field. The `row.dba` expression pulls the business name. The `row.boro` gets the borough. And so on.

Save and check your browser. You'll see all 5,000 records printed out on one page. Way more than we'd ever want to show, but I bet you get the point. One loop was able to print out entire dataset.

### Extracting a RestaurantTable component

Let's clean things up by extracting our table into a reusable component, just like we've done in previous weeks.

Create a new file at `src/lib/components/RestaurantTable.svelte`. Put this in it.

```svelte
<script>
  let { data } = $props();
</script>

<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Restaurant</th>
      <th>Borough</th>
      <th>Cuisine</th>
      <th>Grade</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    {#each data as row}
      <tr>
        <td>{row.dba}</td>
        <td>{row.boro}</td>
        <td>{row.cuisine_description}</td>
        <td>{row.grade}</td>
      </tr>
    {/each}
  </tbody>
</table>

<style>
  table {
    width: 100%;
    border-collapse: collapse;
    font-size: 0.875rem;
  }

  th {
    text-align: left;
    padding: 0.5rem;
    border-bottom: 2px solid var(--color-dark-gray);
    font-weight: bold;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.05em;
  }

  td {
    padding: 0.5rem;
    border-bottom: 1px solid #eee;
  }

  tr:hover {
    background-color: var(--color-light-gray);
  }
</style>
```

Back in `src/routes/+page.svelte`, import the component and replace your table markup:

```js
import RestaurantTable from '$lib/components/RestaurantTable.svelte';
```

```svelte
<RestaurantTable data={data.restaurants} />
```

Save and reload. Similiar result, a little cleaner though.

## Part 4: Summary statistics with `$derived`

A table full of raw data is useful, but it's not a story. Journalists need to summarize — how many restaurants got an A? How many failed? What's the overall picture?

This is where the `$derived` feature from [last week](/docs/coding-the-news/scripts/week-4/#part-3-introduction-to-derived-values) comes back. We used it to calculate streaming subscription totals and tip amounts. Now we'll use it to compute summary statistics from a real dataset.

And we'll display those numbers using the `BigNumber` and `Dashboard` components from [week three](/docs/coding-the-news/scripts/week-3/#part-3-creating-a-parent-component). The tools you've built are starting to work together.

### Counting grades

Open `src/routes/+page.svelte`. In the `<script>` block, add these derived values below your props:

```js
let restaurants = $derived(data.restaurants);
let gradeA = $derived(restaurants.filter(r => r.grade === 'A').length);
let gradeB = $derived(restaurants.filter(r => r.grade === 'B').length);
let gradeC = $derived(restaurants.filter(r => r.grade === 'C').length);
```

The first line creates a convenience variable so we don't have to write `data.restaurants` everywhere. Since it's wrapped in `$derived`, it will stay in sync if we ever change how data flows into the page. We'll modify this variable in Part 4 to incorporate filters.

Each subsequent line uses JavaScript's [`.filter()`](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Array/filter) method to narrow the array to records matching a specific grade, and then [`.length`](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Array/length) to count how many remain.

The expression `r => r.grade === 'A'` is an [arrow function](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Functions/Arrow_functions) — a compact way to write a test. It checks each restaurant record `r` and keeps only the ones where the `grade` field equals `'A'`. The `===` operator is JavaScript's strict equality check.

Because these are wrapped in `$derived()`, Svelte will automatically recalculate them whenever the underlying data changes. That doesn't matter yet — our restaurant array is static. But it will matter a lot in Part 4, when we introduce filters.

### Displaying with BigNumber and Dashboard

Now let's show these counts on the page. Import the components you built in week three:

```js
import BigNumber from '$lib/components/BigNumber.svelte';
import Dashboard from '$lib/components/Dashboard.svelte';
```

Add them to your page above the `RestaurantTable`:

```svelte
<Dashboard>
  <BigNumber
    number={gradeA.toLocaleString()}
    label="Grade A"
  />
  <BigNumber
    number={gradeB.toLocaleString()}
    label="Grade B"
  />
  <BigNumber
    number={gradeC.toLocaleString()}
    label="Grade C"
  />
</Dashboard>
```

We use JavaScript's [`.toLocaleString()`](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Number/toLocaleString) method to format the numbers with commas, so "1234" becomes "1,234." A small touch that makes data easier to read.

Save and check your browser.

You should see three colored boxes showing the grade breakdown, sitting above your full table of restaurants. Already, the shape of a story is emerging. Most restaurants earn an A. Far fewer get a B or C.

Let's add one more statistic to make the picture complete. Add another derived value that calculates the total number of inspections:

```js
let total = $derived(restaurants.length);
```

And add a fourth BigNumber to the dashboard:

```svelte
<Dashboard>
  <BigNumber
    number={total.toLocaleString()}
    label="Inspections"
  />
  <BigNumber
    number={gradeA.toLocaleString()}
    label="Grade A"
  />
  <BigNumber
    number={gradeB.toLocaleString()}
    label="Grade B"
  />
  <BigNumber
    number={gradeC.toLocaleString()}
    label="Grade C"
  />
</Dashboard>
```

These summary statistics aren't just decorative. In a real newsroom, this is how you'd present the top-line findings of your analysis — the numbers that anchor your story and give readers immediate context before they dive into the details.

## Part 5: Filtering with reactive state

We have a table and summary statistics. But they show everything at once. What if a reader only cares about restaurants in their borough? Or wants to see just the places that got a C?

This is where everything we've learned comes together. We'll add dropdown menus that let readers filter the data, and both the table and the summary statistics will update instantly.

### Adding a borough filter

Start by adding a `$state` variable to your script block for the selected borough. We'll default it to an empty string, which will represent "show all boroughs."

```js
let selectedBorough = $state('');
```

Now we need to update our `restaurants` variable to filter the data based on the selection. Replace the simple convenience variable we created in Part 3 with a filtered version:

```js
let restaurants = $derived(
  selectedBorough === ''
    ? data.restaurants
    : data.restaurants.filter(r => r.boro === selectedBorough)
);
```

This reads: if `selectedBorough` is empty, use the full array. Otherwise, keep only restaurants where the `boro` field matches the selection.

Because the grade counts and total are derived from `restaurants`, they'll automatically recalculate when the filter changes. That's the reactive chain doing its job — we only have to change the data in one place, and everything downstream updates.

Now add a `<select>` dropdown to your page, above the Dashboard. We'll use `bind:value` to connect it to our state variable, just like we did with the tip calculator [last week](/docs/coding-the-news/scripts/week-4/#part-4-introduction-to-binding).

```svelte
<div class="filters">
  <label for="borough">Borough</label>
  <select id="borough" bind:value={selectedBorough}>
    <option value="">All boroughs</option>
    <option value="Manhattan">Manhattan</option>
    <option value="Brooklyn">Brooklyn</option>
    <option value="Queens">Queens</option>
    <option value="Bronx">Bronx</option>
    <option value="Staten Island">Staten Island</option>
  </select>
</div>
```

And update the table to use our filtered `restaurants` variable:

```svelte
<RestaurantTable data={restaurants} />
```

Save and check your browser. Select "Brooklyn" from the dropdown.

The summary stats recalculate. The table shrinks to show only Brooklyn restaurants. Switch to "Queens" and it all updates again. Select "All boroughs" and you're back to the full dataset.

This is the reactive chain in action: the user changes the dropdown, which updates `selectedBorough`, which recalculates `restaurants`, which recalculates the grade counts and the total, which updates the BigNumbers and the table. All of that happens automatically because of `$state` and `$derived`.

### Adding a cuisine filter

Let's add a second dropdown for cuisine type. This works the same way, but since there are dozens of cuisine types in the data, we'll generate the options dynamically from the dataset itself.

Add another state variable:

```js
let selectedCuisine = $state('');
```

We'll need a list of all the unique cuisine types in the data. Add a derived value that extracts them:

```js
let cuisines = $derived(
  [...new Set(data.restaurants.map(r => r.cuisine_description))].sort()
);
```

This line does a few things at once. The `.map()` method pulls the `cuisine_description` from every record. Wrapping it in `new Set()` eliminates duplicates, since a Set can only contain unique values. The `[...]` [spread syntax](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Operators/Spread_syntax) converts the Set back into an array. And `.sort()` puts them in alphabetical order.

Notice we derive the cuisine list from `data.restaurants` — the full, unfiltered dataset — so the dropdown always shows all available cuisines regardless of the current borough selection.

Now update the `restaurants` variable to account for both dropdowns:

```js
let restaurants = $derived(
  data.restaurants.filter(r => {
    if (selectedBorough !== '' && r.boro !== selectedBorough) return false;
    if (selectedCuisine !== '' && r.cuisine_description !== selectedCuisine) return false;
    return true;
  })
);
```

This filter checks each restaurant against both criteria. If a borough is selected and the restaurant doesn't match, it's excluded. Same for cuisine. If both are empty, everything passes through. If both are set, only restaurants matching both survive.

Add the second dropdown next to the first:

```svelte
<div class="filters">
  <label for="borough">Borough</label>
  <select id="borough" bind:value={selectedBorough}>
    <option value="">All boroughs</option>
    <option value="Manhattan">Manhattan</option>
    <option value="Brooklyn">Brooklyn</option>
    <option value="Queens">Queens</option>
    <option value="Bronx">Bronx</option>
    <option value="Staten Island">Staten Island</option>
  </select>

  <label for="cuisine">Cuisine</label>
  <select id="cuisine" bind:value={selectedCuisine}>
    <option value="">All cuisines</option>
    {#each cuisines as cuisine}
      <option value={cuisine}>{cuisine}</option>
    {/each}
  </select>
</div>
```

Notice we're using `{#each}` again — this time to generate `<option>` tags from our list of cuisines. The same tool that renders table rows can render any repeating element.

Add some styles to keep the filters tidy. You can add these to a `<style>` block at the bottom of `+page.svelte`, or put them in a new component if you prefer.

```svelte
<style>
  .filters {
    display: flex;
    gap: 1rem;
    align-items: end;
    margin: 1.5rem 0;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
  }

  label {
    display: block;
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    font-weight: bold;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.05em;
    margin-bottom: 0.25rem;
  }

  select {
    padding: 0.5rem;
    border: 1px solid #ccc;
    font-size: 1rem;
  }
</style>
```

Save and check your browser. Try different combinations — Mexican restaurants in the Bronx, Italian restaurants in Manhattan, pizza places across all boroughs. The stats and table update with each selection.

If a combination returns no results, you'll see the "No restaurants found" message we added with `{:else}` in Part 2. That fallback is doing its job.

### The full picture

Let's step back and appreciate what you've built. In a single session, you've gone from a raw government dataset to an interactive data application. Readers can explore restaurant inspection results by borough and cuisine, guided by summary statistics that update in real time.

The reactive chain flows from the user's selections through your `$state` variables, into the `$derived` filter, through the `$derived` summary counts, and out to the components on the page. Every link in that chain is something you learned in the past five weeks:

| Concept | Week learned | Role in this project |
|---------|-------------|---------------------|
| Components and props | [Week 3](/docs/coding-the-news/scripts/week-3) | RestaurantTable, BigNumber, Dashboard |
| `$state` | [Week 4](/docs/coding-the-news/scripts/week-4) | Tracking the selected borough and cuisine |
| `$derived` | [Week 4](/docs/coding-the-news/scripts/week-4) | Filtered data, grade counts, total |
| `bind:value` | [Week 4](/docs/coding-the-news/scripts/week-4) | Connecting dropdowns to state |
| `{#each}` | Today | Rendering table rows and dropdown options |
| `+page.js` load function | Today | Getting data into the page |

This is the foundation of every interactive data story you'll encounter in the wild. The datasets will be different. The designs will be more polished. But the architecture — data loaded by the framework, state tracking user input, derived values driving the display — is the same.

## Homework

### Task 1: Build a data explorer

Find a different dataset on the [NYC Open Data portal](https://data.cityofnewyork.us/). Here are some good ones to explore:

- [311 Service Requests](https://data.cityofnewyork.us/Social-Services/311-Service-Requests-from-2010-to-Present/erm2-nwe9) — complaints about noise, potholes, rodents and more
- [Film Permits](https://data.cityofnewyork.us/City-Government/Film-Permits/tg4x-b46p) — where movies and TV shows are shooting around the city
- [Popular Baby Names](https://data.cityofnewyork.us/Health/Popular-Baby-Names/25th-nujf) — the most popular baby names by year, ethnicity and gender
- [Motor Vehicle Collisions](https://data.cityofnewyork.us/Public-Safety/Motor-Vehicle-Collisions-Crashes/h9gi-nx95) — traffic crash data with location and contributing factors

Adapt the fetch script we wrote in class to download a subset of your chosen dataset. Change the API URL, the `$where` filter, and the `$select` fields to match your dataset's columns.

Import the data into a fresh copy of our class template using a `+page.js` load function. Build a table with `{#each}`, add at least one summary statistic with `$derived` and `BigNumber`, and create at least one dropdown filter.

Deploy to GitHub Pages and send me the link.

### Task 2: Prepare to present

Be ready to share your data explorer with the class. You should be prepared to:

- Explain why you chose this dataset and what story it could tell
- Walk through how you adapted the fetch script
- Show the reactive chain: what state do you track, what derived values depend on it, and how do the components update
- Discuss how you used AI in the process and what you had to figure out yourself
